definition_long:
The canonical winning position of the rook-and-pawn-versus-rook ending, and the one the stronger side steers for. Its characteristics are exact:

  - the pawn is any pawn EXCEPT a rook pawn;
  - the pawn stands on the seventh rank;
  - the attacking king is on the queening square, in its own pawn's way;
  - the attacking rook cuts the defending king off by at least one file;
  - the defending rook is on the file on the far side of the pawn.

The win is by BUILDING A BRIDGE: a rook check drives the king back, the rook drops to the fourth rank, and when the defender checks from the side the rook interposes, shielding the king so it can walk out and the pawn can promote.

Karsten Müller has called it possibly the most important position in endgame theory, and de la Villa pairs it with the Philidor as the two that matter most. Rook endings occur in roughly 8-10% of all games and reduce to one or the other more often than to anything else.

THE NAME IS WRONG, AND THE ERROR IS TRACEABLE. The position does not appear in Luis Ramírez de Lucena's Repetición de Amores e Arte de Axedrez (1497). The earliest preserved discussion is in Alessandro Salvio's Il Puttino (1634), and Salvio attributes it to Scipione Genovino. The misattribution most likely entered via the sixth edition of the Handbuch des Schachspiels (1880), where the editor Constantin Schwede credited it to 'Lucena 96', apparently through confusion over references in van der Linde's 1874 work. The name is now standard and should keep being used — but it should not be presented as telling you who found it.
